Here is the code snippet demonstrating the proper use of the Java API to avoid vulnerabilities:

```java
// This code snippet demonstrates a secure use of the Java API
// by using prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.

import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class GoodCode {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydatabase";
        String username = "myuser";
        String password = "mypassword";

        try {
            Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password);
            System.out.println("Connected to the database");

            // Using a prepared statement to prevent SQL injection
            String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
            PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(query);

            // Sanitizing the input to prevent SQL injection
            pstmt.setString(1, "sanitizedUsername");

            ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();
            while (rs.next()) {
                System.out.println("Username: " + rs.getString("username"));
            }

            rs.close();
            pstmt.close();
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Failed to connect to the database: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

In the good code snippet, we use a prepared statement to prevent SQL injection attacks by sanitizing the input before executing the query. This is a more secure way to interact with a database in Java.

